From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: il97680a: the three commands the vendor ends with

The stock Android install is still on the device, so the panel's
qualified init sequence could be read rather than guessed at. It is in
dtbo_a, as qcom,mdss-dsi-on-command on the node named
"il97680a amoled panel without DSC", and this driver already matches
its first fourteen commands byte for byte - the same register pages,
the same 0x0d 0x75 0x00 0x00 brightness, the same 120ms and 20ms waits.

It ends with three more that this driver does not send, after the
display is already on:

    ff 5a a5 22      select register page 0x22
    e1 01            register 0xe1 = 0x01
    ff 5a a5 00      back to page 0x00

What 0xe1 on page 0x22 does is not documented anywhere available, and
the panel has worked without it, so this is not a fix for anything
named. It is sent because the vendor sends it and because being three
commands short of the sequence the panel is qualified with is not a
position to defend - a register written after display-on, once, in a
vendor page, is the shape of a compensation or emission setting, and
those are exactly the ones whose absence does not announce itself.

Also recorded from that node, since nothing in this tree knew it: the
panel is an AMOLED, the vendor drives it in command mode with a
hardware TE pin where this driver uses video mode, and its own two
timings are 1302 x 998 at 60 and 120Hz - the same htotal this driver
uses, three fewer lines of vertical back porch.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
@@ -167,6 +167,15 @@
 	mipi_dsi_msleep(&dsi_ctx, 20);
 
+	/*
+	 * The vendor's sequence ends here, with the display already on. Read
+	 * from the stock dtbo_a, whose first fourteen commands this function
+	 * already matches exactly.
+	 */
+	mipi_dsi_dcs_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0xff, 0x5a, 0xa5, 0x22);
+	mipi_dsi_dcs_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0xe1, 0x01);
+	mipi_dsi_dcs_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0xff, 0x5a, 0xa5, 0x00);
+
 	return dsi_ctx.accum_err;
 }
 
